## Authentication (AWS Cognito)

The CDK stack owns the Cognito user pool, the app client and the managed login domain
(`{{cognitoSettings.domainPrefix}}-<env>.auth.{{awsEnv.region}}.amazoncognito.com`). Users are invited by an
administrator; self sign-up is disabled.

Deployed builds read the Cognito IDs from `/config.json`, which the stack writes at deploy time, so
one build works for every environment.

### Local development

The staging app client also accepts `http://localhost:3010` as a callback URL. Copy the staging stack
outputs into `.env`:

```sh
aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name {{project.name}}-staging \
    --query "Stacks[0].Outputs[?starts_with(OutputKey, 'Cognito')]" --output table
```

| Output | `.env` variable |
|---|---|
| `CognitoRegion` | `VITE_APP_COGNITO_REGION` |
| `CognitoUserPoolId` | `VITE_APP_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID` |
| `CognitoClientId` | `VITE_APP_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID` |
| `CognitoDomain` | `VITE_APP_COGNITO_DOMAIN` |
| `CognitoScope` | `VITE_APP_COGNITO_SCOPE` |
{%- if features contains "cognito-admin" %}
| `CognitoIdentityPoolId` | `VITE_APP_COGNITO_IDENTITY_POOL_ID` |
{%- endif %}

Tokens are stored in `localStorage`, the same as the Auth0 setup, so a session survives reloads
and new tabs. The tradeoff is that any script running on the page can read them, so an XSS bug
would expose them.

### API

The API verifies access tokens against the `CognitoIssuer` output and this app's `CognitoClientId`.
To manage users from the API, grant its role access to `CognitoUserPoolArn`. Set `apiIdentifier`
in `cdk/src/env-*.ts` to add a resource server with an `access` scope, which the app then requests
at sign-in.

### Roles

Roles are Cognito groups. They appear in the `cognito:groups` claim of both the ID and the access
token, which is what the API should authorize against.
{%- if features contains "cognito-admin" %}

### User administration

Members of the `admin` group see an **Admin** link in the app bar. It opens `/admin`, where they can
invite, edit, disable and delete users, reset passwords and MFA, assign roles, and see who has
each role.

There is no backend involved: the app exchanges the administrator's ID token at a Cognito identity
pool for temporary AWS credentials and calls the Cognito API directly. Only the `admin` group is
mapped to an IAM role, and that role may only manage this user pool. Everybody else is denied
credentials.

Roles themselves are defined in CDK, next to the `admin` group in `cdk/src/cognito-auth.ts`; the
admin UI cannot create, edit or delete them.

### First administrator

The first administrator has to be created from the command line, after the first deploy:

```sh
USER_POOL_ID=<CognitoUserPoolId output>

aws cognito-idp admin-create-user --user-pool-id "$USER_POOL_ID" \
    --username admin@example.com \
    --user-attributes Name=email,Value=admin@example.com Name=email_verified,Value=true \
    --desired-delivery-mediums EMAIL

aws cognito-idp admin-add-user-to-group --user-pool-id "$USER_POOL_ID" \
    --username admin@example.com --group-name admin
```

They receive an email with a temporary password and can manage everybody else from the app.
{%- endif %}

### Login page branding

The managed login pages use Cognito's default style with `src/assets/favicon.svg` as the logo (see
`ManagedLoginBranding` in `cdk/src/cognito-auth.ts`). To customize it further, edit the style in the
Cognito console's branding editor, then export it and copy the `Settings` part of the output into
the construct:

```sh
aws cognito-idp describe-managed-login-branding-by-client --user-pool-id <pool> \
    --client-id <client> --return-merged-resources
```

### Emails

The emails Cognito sends are HTML templates in `cdk/emails`, wrapped in the shared
`layout.html` when the stack is synthesized:

| Template | Sent for |
|---|---|
| `invitation.html` | New users invited by an administrator, and resent invitations |
| `verification-code.html` | Forgot password, password resets started by an administrator, and confirming a changed email address |

Cognito uses one template for all verification codes, so its wording has to fit every case.

### Email delivery

By default Cognito sends email itself, which is limited to 50 emails per day. Set `ses` in
`cdk/src/env-*.ts` before going live.

Verify the from address in SES first, or set `verifiedDomain` when its domain is already verified.
Cognito drops invitations and password resets sent from an unverified identity, without an error.
New AWS accounts start in the SES sandbox and can only email verified addresses, so request
production access before inviting real users.
